# Mutation cases for a DELAYED host-served Event I/O Processor send.
#
# Run with
# `scripts/mutate sce-build/tests/mutations/host_processor_delayed_dispatch.cases`.
#
# `host_processor_dispatch.cases` studies what happens when a declared `<send>`
# reaches the engine. This one studies what happens when the document told the
# engine to WAIT first — W3C SCXML 6.2.4 puts the delay before the dispatch and
# says nothing about which processor the send named, and 6.2.5 makes that set
# open, so a host-served send carrying a delay is an ordinary delayed send whose
# delivery happens to be somebody else's.
#
# It was not. Every backend chose the host branch ahead of the delay branch in
# one `elif` chain per language, so the act was performed at the instant the
# block ran and `delay` was discarded — while the manifest went on answering
# `needs_event_scheduler: true`, telling the host to drive with `tick` for a
# wait the engine had already thrown away. The suite these cases attack is what
# now says otherwise, and the mutations are the ways it could stop saying it:
#
#   * the wait is not honoured (the original defect, restored);
#   * the wait is honoured somewhere `<cancel>` cannot reach, so a cancelled
#     act still reaches the host — which no configuration assertion can undo,
#     because the side effect is the point of the act;
#   * the deferred act is dropped instead of performed;
#   * nobody performed it and nothing said so;
#   * the deadline query cannot see it, so a host sleeping on the answer sleeps
#     straight past the act.
#
# Every case is a RUNTIME mutation, so no tree is regenerated: the committed
# machine in `sce-rust-tests` calls into the engine, and what changes is what
# the engine does with the entry.
#
# Anchors are literal slices of the source; an edit that moves one is reported
# INCONCLUSIVE with the anchor quoted, never as a pass.

mutation_tests -p sce-rust-tests --test delayed_host_send
mutation_targets backends/rust/runtime/src/engine.rs

# ── The wait is not honoured ────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The defect itself, put back the only way the runtime can express it: the
# entry is armed for an instant that has already passed, so the first tick
# performs it. `a_host_served_send_waits_for_its_delay` is what catches it —
# the 100 ms `probe` was supposed to arrive first.

mutation_case "the deferred send is armed for a deadline already past" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/runtime/src/engine.rs",
     """        let ready_at = self.sched_now_plus(delay);
        self.scheduler
            .schedule_host_send_at(request, ready_at, send_id)""",
     """        let _ = delay;
        let ready_at = self.sched_now_plus(Duration::from_millis(0));
        self.scheduler
            .schedule_host_send_at(request, ready_at, send_id)""")
PY

# ── …or is honoured out of reach of `<cancel>` ──────────────────────
#
# W3C SCXML 6.3 drops a delayed send that has not been dispatched. An engine
# that kept the deferred act anywhere else — a side list, a timer thread —
# would still pass the case above and fail here, which is exactly why the
# fixture measures both. The witness is host-side: the handler must never be
# asked to perform the cancelled act at all.

mutation_case "a cancel passes over a deferred host send" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/runtime/src/engine.rs",
     "        self.entries.retain(|e| !e.send_id.matches(send_id));",
     """        self.entries
            .retain(|e| !e.send_id.matches(send_id) || matches!(e.act, ScheduledAct::HostSend(_)));""")
PY

# ── The deferred act never happens ──────────────────────────────────
#
# The wait is honoured and then nothing is performed. From the document's side
# this is the worst of the three: it waited, it was told nothing, and the reply
# it is waiting for has nobody to come from.
#
# Aimed inside the dispatch rather than at the tick arm that calls it. Emptying
# that arm leaves `perform_deferred_host_send` with no caller, and this
# workspace's deny-warnings build rejects a private method nothing calls — the
# tree stops compiling and the round reports INCONCLUSIVE instead of a verdict.
# Measured 2026-08-24, not guessed. Refusing the dispatch its answer drops the
# act just as completely and keeps every symbol referenced.

mutation_case "the deferred act never reaches the host" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/runtime/src/engine.rs",
     """        let served = self.perform_host_send(request);
        if served.is_none() && !self.has_event_processor(&processor_type) {""",
     """        let _ = request;
        let served: Option<Vec<crate::host_processor::HostSendResponse>> = None;
        if served.is_none() && !self.has_event_processor(&processor_type) {""")
PY

# ── …or happens, unserved, in silence ───────────────────────────────
#
# The immediate site raises §scxml-6.2's `error.execution` for a declared type
# nobody registered. The deferred one cannot — it returned when the send was
# armed — so the engine owes the report at the deadline. Without it a wiring
# mistake on a delayed send is perfect silence.
#
# Aimed at the NAME rather than at the `raise` call: the name is what the
# engine has instead of the document's enum, and looking it up under a name no
# machine declares drops the report as completely while keeping every type
# inferred, so the tree still compiles and the round still returns a verdict.

mutation_case "the report is looked up under a name no machine declares" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/runtime/src/engine.rs",
     """            if let Some(evt) = P::get_event_from_name("error.execution") {""",
     """            if let Some(evt) = P::get_event_from_name("") {""")
PY

mutation_case "an unregistered type reports as served at the deadline" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/runtime/src/engine.rs",
     """        if served.is_none() && !self.has_event_processor(&processor_type) {""",
     """        if served.is_none() && self.has_event_processor(&processor_type) {""")
PY

# ── The host is never told when to come back ────────────────────────
#
# A host driving on `time_until_next_scheduled_ms` sleeps on that answer. An
# act the query cannot see is an act the host sleeps straight past — the
# machine is correct and never gets a tick in which to prove it.

mutation_case "the next-deadline query cannot see a deferred host send" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/runtime/src/engine.rs",
     "        self.entries.iter().map(|e| e.ready_at).min()",
     """        self.entries
            .iter()
            .filter(|e| !matches!(e.act, ScheduledAct::HostSend(_)))
            .map(|e| e.ready_at)
            .min()""")
PY
